<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">South China Sea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> Sea is a marginal sea of the Pacific bordered by southern China, mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. It carried maritime trade between China and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a> note that Srivijaya controlled shipping between the Indian Ocean and the South China Sea, and accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a> discuss maritime commerce through it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/south-china-sea.jpg" alt="South China Sea" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Serg!o · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mar_de_China_Meridional_-_BM_WMS_2004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ayutthaya's wealth rested on wet-rice agriculture in the flood plain of the Chao Phraya and on its position as an entrepôt between the Indian Ocean and the <b>South China Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Champa grew out of the Sa Huỳnh culture, an Austronesian seafaring people settled on the coast of present-day central Vietnam between about 1000 BCE and 200 CE, who traded across the <b>South China Sea</b> with the Philippine archipelago and Taiwan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2013 security forces expelled armed followers of a claimant to the Sultanate of Sulu who landed at Lahad Datu in Sabah, and Malaysia maintains claims and patrols in the <b>South China Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…1990s rapid economic growth funded sweeping modernisation: China commissioned its first aircraft carrier, the Liaoning, in 2012, built artificial islands in the <b>South China Sea</b> from about 2014, restructured its forces in 2015–16 and by the 2020s had the largest navy in the world by number of ships.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Since the 2000s Vietnam has modernised its navy and air force with Russian equipment, including six Kilo-class submarines, to defend its claims in the <b>South China Sea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Palembang on the Musi River in southern Sumatra was the empire's core capital, strategically placed to control shipping moving between the Indian Ocean and the <b>South China Sea</b> through the Malacca and Sunda straits.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
